     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.** Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg a day.\n- The maximum single prescription dose is usually 800 mg, so 1,600 mg is double that.\n- The maximum daily dose is usually 3,200 mg, so 6,400 mg is double that too.\n- Doses this high significantly raise the risk of GI bleeding, acute kidney injury, and heart problems, even in a healthy 70 kg adult with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n**2. A prescriber needs to place it.** Medication orders have to come from a licensed clinician who is responsible for the patient's care, such as the attending, a resident, an NP, or a PA. Since you're not a clinician, I can't enter it on your behalf, even with a corrected dose. The prescriber's name is what makes the order valid and accountable.\n\n**What you can do:**\n- Ask the patient's medicine team to review the order. They may have meant 400 mg or 800 mg four times daily, or 1,600 mg as a total daily dose. A pharmacist can also help clarify.\n- When the treating clinician confirms the dose, they can enter it, or ask me to enter it under their name.\n\nIf there's something else I can help with, like drafting a message to the care team, just let me know."
      }
     ],
